        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 3.1 Why a third-moment objective",
            "block_bbox": [
                138,
                572,
                648,
                604
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    138.0,
                    572.0
                ],
                [
                    648.0,
                    572.0
                ],
                [
                    648.0,
                    604.0
                ],
                [
                    138.0,
                    604.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Suppose a node contains two regimes, each a local linear map from X to Y with Gaussian scatter, in proportions  $ \\pi $ and  $ 1 - \\pi $. Along any direction the marginal score distribution is then a two-component Gaussian mixture, which is non-Gaussian whenever the component means differ. The signature of that non-Gaussianity is carried by the higher-order moments: a two-component mixture is, in general, both skewed and heavy-tailed relative to a single Gaussian, and the skewness in particular encodes the asymmetry between the two components. The direction that best separates the regimes is therefore often a direction of large joint skewness, and it need not coincide with – and may be orthogonal to – the direction of large joint covariance. A covariance-based objective such as (2) cannot, by construction, prefer such a direction; a third-moment objective can. This is the motivation for a twoblock decomposition driven by coskeewness, the natural third-order cross-moment between the predictor and response scores.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                620,
                1083,
                965
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    620.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    620.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    965.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    965.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "At this point, it is noted that dimension reduction based on higher order (co-)moments has been explored before.. For instance, a kurtosis based one-block dimension reduction was illustrated to better reveal cluster in analytical chemical data (Driscoll et al., 2020). Likewise, a criterion that accommodates for higher order comoments was adopted to calculate generalized betas for financial portfolios (Serneels, 2019). However, in both those approaches, a computationally involved projection pursuit algorithm was used. This article is first to extend the use of higher order co-moments into the twoblock simultaneous dimension reduction framework and it is also first to do so based on a computationally very efficient matrix-free power method, as will bne explained in Subsection 3.3.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                966,
                1084,
                1227
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
